@meta
id: 23-14
title: Reflow a comment without eating the code
chapter: 23
type: lesson
skills: format-gq
par: 26
minutes: 5
vscode: Rewrap handles comment leaders too; Alt-Q
open: refund.provider.ts
vimrc: set textwidth=72

@brief
Two comments in this file need rewrapping to 72 columns. The header
comment is one very long line with a blank line after it. The comment
above `isInsideWindow` is three ragged lines, and there is no blank
line between it and the method.

Reflow both. Every reflowed line must start with `//`, and the code
must not move by a character.

@keys
| `gqip` | reflow the paragraph — safe when a blank line follows |
| `gq2j` | reflow this line and the two below — safe when it does not |
| `gqj` | reflow this line and the next |
| `V` … `gq` | reflow exactly the selected lines |

@hints
The header is a paragraph on its own: `gqip` from line 1.
--
The second block is not on its own. `ip` from a comment that touches
the code below it includes the code, and `gq` will happily wrap a
method into a comment. Give `gq` a motion that covers only the three
comment lines.
--
`/Work out` then `gq2j`. Or `Vjj` then `gq` if you want to see what
you are about to format.

@solution
    gqip /Work out<Enter> gq2j :wq<Enter>

Twenty-two keystrokes.

Vim's TypeScript filetype sets `comments` so that formatting knows
`//` is a leader: it strips the leader from every line, reflows the
text, and puts the leader back at the start of every new line. That
is the `q` flag in `formatoptions`, and it is on for you. The same
works for `#` in YAML and shell, `--` in SQL, and `*` in a block
comment, though the block comment sometimes loses a space of
alignment; `//` lines are the safe bet.

What `gq` does not know is the difference between prose and code. It
formats whatever lines you hand it, and a paragraph — in the text
object sense — is simply lines with no blank line between them. A
comment sitting directly on top of a method is in the same paragraph
as the method. So the rule is: `gqip` when there is a blank line
under the comment, a counted motion or a visual selection when there
is not. Try `gqip` on the second block and `u` it back if you want to
see how much damage one keystroke can do.

Notice the first line of the header comment: 72 columns exactly.
`textwidth` is a limit, not a target, and `gq` fills each line as far
as it can.

@start refund.provider.ts
// Refund window rules. Orders can be refunded for thirty days after they are placed. Past that point cancelling still works, but no money moves and the reason is recorded on the order for finance to reconcile at month end.

import { injectable, inject } from 'tsyringe';
import { OrderRepository } from '../order/order.repository';
import { PaymentService } from '../../infrastructure/services/payment.service';

@injectable()
export class RefundProvider {
  constructor(
    @inject(OrderRepository) protected orderRepository: OrderRepository,
    @inject(PaymentService) protected paymentService: PaymentService,
  ) {}

  // Work out whether an order is still inside the refund window. The window is measured from placedAt,
  // not from the last status change,
  // because finance reconcile against the original order date and nothing else.
  public isInsideWindow(placedAt: Date) {
    return Date.now() - placedAt.getTime() <= 30 * 86_400_000;
  }

  public async refund(orderId: string) {
    const order = await this.orderRepository.findOne({ where: { id: orderId } });

    if (!order || !this.isInsideWindow(order.placedAt)) {
      return this.orderRepository.update({
        where: { id: orderId },
        values: { cancellationReason: 'REFUND_WINDOW_EXPIRED' },
      });
    }

    return this.paymentService.refund(order.id);
  }
}

@expected refund.provider.ts
// Refund window rules. Orders can be refunded for thirty days after
// they are placed. Past that point cancelling still works, but no money
// moves and the reason is recorded on the order for finance to
// reconcile at month end.

import { injectable, inject } from 'tsyringe';
import { OrderRepository } from '../order/order.repository';
import { PaymentService } from '../../infrastructure/services/payment.service';

@injectable()
export class RefundProvider {
  constructor(
    @inject(OrderRepository) protected orderRepository: OrderRepository,
    @inject(PaymentService) protected paymentService: PaymentService,
  ) {}

  // Work out whether an order is still inside the refund window. The
  // window is measured from placedAt, not from the last status change,
  // because finance reconcile against the original order date and
  // nothing else.
  public isInsideWindow(placedAt: Date) {
    return Date.now() - placedAt.getTime() <= 30 * 86_400_000;
  }

  public async refund(orderId: string) {
    const order = await this.orderRepository.findOne({ where: { id: orderId } });

    if (!order || !this.isInsideWindow(order.placedAt)) {
      return this.orderRepository.update({
        where: { id: orderId },
        values: { cancellationReason: 'REFUND_WINDOW_EXPIRED' },
      });
    }

    return this.paymentService.refund(order.id);
  }
}
